{{rfe|en}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# To [[surround]] or [[guard]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1796| title=The Scottish Register |author=| page=252| passage=This was executed with sch gallantry and spirit by the troops, that, notwithstanding the natural strength of this pos, the abbatis of fruit trees that were made, the batteries of the town of Bommel which '''stanked''' the approach, and the considerable number of men who defended it, it was soon carried, and the enemy driven across the river (every where passable on the ice) with considerable loss of men and of four pieces of cannon.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1815| title=Memorie of the Somervilles|author=James Somerville Baron Somerville; Walter Scott | page=384| passage=upon the south of the garden, by ane easy descent, you come to the great orch-yaird containing sex aikers of ground, includeing a parcell thereof that is woody, all upon one levine, '''stanked''' and hedged, about whose back entry from the south leads you first to a triangle haugh surrounded with wood, and then to the river of Clyde for salmond fishing for more nor two mylles, which makes the lenth of the wholl barronie from Garingill to the Miregill mouth.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1830| title=The History of the Troubles and Memorable Transactions in Scotland From the Year 1624 to 1645|author=John Spalding | page=490| passage=Upon Friday the 25th of April, sir William Forbes of Craigievar, at his own hand, takes in the place of Kemnay, frae the widow lady thereof, plants some soldiers therein, being '''stanked''' about, and of good defence;}}

===Etymology 6===
{{rfe|en}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|Cornwall}} To [[trample]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1873| title=Foolish Dick: an Autobiography of Richard Hampton |author=Richard Hampton | page=43| passage=In the por ( bustle ) I lost my hat ; tell gittin ' cloase to a mait-stannin ' (shambles), to saave myself from bein' '''stanked''' ( trampled ) under fut, I got up and set down 'pon the stannin' ; an ' then, aw, I feelt my sawl all a-fire weth love for everybody theere, and sprengin' to my feet, I begun to ex'ort, and then took to pray.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1898|work=The Cornish Magazine |title=Recollections of a Parish Worker|author=M|volume=1|page=471|passage=And then she took down the cloam from the chimbly and '''stanked''' it under her feet, so as no one shouldn't hev it after she war gone.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1906| title=My Cornish Neighbours |author=Mrs. Havelock Ellis | page=40| passage=I like to know a man be '''stanked''' on, for they'm miserable torments most o' their time .  }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1909 | title=In Spite of Fate |author=Silas Kitto Hocking | page=177| passage= I caan't afford to ' ave my straw '''stanked''' down an ' spoiled. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1931 | title=Storms and Tea-cups |author=Mrs. Alfred Sidgwick | page=93| passage=I remarked on this to Tippet and he mumbled something about they saucy girls having gathered all the eating peas and '''stanked''' on his lettuces and carrots to get them.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2000 | title=The Literature of Cornwall|author=Alan M. Kent | page=269| passage=It is this moment which Parker chooses to tell; in so doing setting a new agenda and standard for Cornu-English dialect literature: ' We should ha' just '''stanked''' on over the top of 'en but instead we hesitated and squared up.}}
# {{lb|en|Cornwall}} To [[stumble]] or [[lurch]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1895| title=Randigal Rhymes and a Glossary of Cornish Words |author=Joseph Thomas | page=24| passage=I put on my clean gook to-day, And went to fetch some barm,When I '''stanked''' 'pon a slaw-cripple, Down there by Hodge's farm. }}
#* {{quote-journal|en|year=1901|work=Pearson's Magazine |title=|author=|volume=11|page=362|passage=But she '''stanked''' upon a wuilkin one day in the chall, and after that she was always liable to quames.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1975 | title=Confession of a Rebel |author=Jack R. Clemo | page=72| passage=He catched sight o' the barra handles sticked up over a lime bucket, and '''stanked''' fore to grab 'em. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2007| title=Making Moonta: The Invention of Australia's Little Cornwall |author=Philip Payton | page=122| passage=Certainly, there would be those happy to support his elevation to parliament: Then up jumped Reuben Gill, And he '''stanked''' on the floor To send John Prisk to Parliament He'd beg from door to door. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2010| title=Angletwitch and Poppydock |author=George Pritchard | page=9| passage=The wemmen and some of the men sot down, too, but all the young cheldern runned away down out o' sight, so I '''stanked''' out as fast as I cud to see where they was going to, and when I got there, they was all down by a will.}}

===Etymology 7===
{{rfe|en}}

====Verb====
{{en-verb}}

# {{lb|en|dairying}} To cause (the udders) to become blocked and inflamed from lack of [[milk]]ing.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1913| title=Journal of the Royal Sanitary Institute - Volume 33 |author=Royal Sanitary Institute (Great Britain) | page=86| passage=In cattle auctions, cows are frequently seen with “'''stanked''' udders,” when a cow is driven to market several miles along hard roads with a loaded udder, its milk is not improved for human consumption. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1950| title=The Country Companion|author=Frederick Daniel Smith; Barbara Wilcox | page=91| passage=An old cow, giving rather little milk and perhaps not freshly calved, may be ''''stanked'''' in order to deceive a prospective purchaser, and in this case an accusation of 'cruelty' would not be so ill-founded.}}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1950 | title=Sold for Two Farthings|author=Frederick Daniel Smith; Barbara Wilcox | page=122| passage=The cows was '''stanked''' and the calves was empty. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2013| title=History of Hereford Cattle|author=James MacDonald | page=| passage=also the advantage of preventing any of the quarters of the udder from becoming '''stanked''', from not being used by the calf, which leads in time to the quarter being lost. }}
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2022| title=Precious Bane|author=Mary Webb | page=| passage=So instead of pleading for Beguildy I said—“Look's cows coming, they'll be '''stanked''' if they inna milked.”}}

